From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GloriousEggroll <gloriouseggroll@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 09:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] winex11: Poll resize completion with a busy message queue

move_resize_window polls XQueryPointer for button release, but first drains
the entire Win32 message queue between polls. If an application renders in
a WM_TIMER callback that takes longer than its timer interval, that queue
never empties. Wine stays inside SC_SIZE and never sends WM_EXITSIZEMOVE,
even though the window manager has finished resizing the window.

This occurs with Aion and Wine-drawn borders: WM_ENTERSIZEMOVE starts a
20 ms timer, callbacks take about 32 ms, and the resize command remains
on the stack until the window is destroyed roughly 100 seconds later.
The client grows from 800x600 to 1363x961 without a D3D9 backbuffer reset.

Bound message dispatch by the existing 100 ms pointer polling interval,
and wait only for the remainder of that interval. This preserves message
filtering and synthetic button release while allowing resize completion
to be checked even if the application continually generates messages.

This only fixes starvation in the X11 driver-initiated move/resize loop.
It does not change server-decoration-initiated sizing, Wayland sizing,
mouse coordinate mapping, or D3D9 reset behavior.

---
diff --git a/dlls/winex11.drv/mouse.c b/dlls/winex11.drv/mouse.c
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/mouse.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/mouse.c
@@ -1552,6 +1552,7 @@ void move_resize_window( HWND hwnd, int dir, POINT pos )
         MSG msg;
         INPUT input;
         int x, y, rootX, rootY;
+        DWORD start = NtGetTickCount(), elapsed;
 
         if (!XQueryPointer( display, root_window, &root, &child, &rootX, &rootY, &x, &y, &xstate )) break;
 
@@ -1576,10 +1577,13 @@ void move_resize_window( HWND hwnd, int dir, POINT pos )
                 NtUserTranslateMessage( &msg, 0 );
                 NtUserDispatchMessage( &msg );
             }
+            /* A rendering timer can keep the queue busy indefinitely. */
+            if (NtGetTickCount() - start >= 100) break;
         }
 
         if (!(xstate & (Button1Mask << (button - 1)))) break;
-        NtUserMsgWaitForMultipleObjectsEx( 0, NULL, 100, QS_ALLINPUT, 0 );
+        if ((elapsed = NtGetTickCount() - start) < 100)
+            NtUserMsgWaitForMultipleObjectsEx( 0, NULL, 100 - elapsed, QS_ALLINPUT, 0 );
     }
 
     TRACE( "hwnd %p/%lx done\n", hwnd, win );
